<h2>Always-On Strategies (Run These 365 Days a Year)</h2>
<p>These eight strategies form your marketing foundation. They work in every season and compound over time. Stop any of them and your lead flow drops within 30 days.</p>
<hr />
<h3>Strategy 1: Optimize Google Business Profile for Roofing Keywords</h3>
<p><strong>Best Season:</strong> Always-On<br />
<strong>Expected Lead Volume:</strong> 15–40 qualified calls per month<br />
<strong>Cost to Implement:</strong> $0–$500 (DIY) or $800–$1,500/month (agency-managed)</p>
<h4>How to Implement</h4>
<p>Your Google Business Profile (GBP) is the single highest-ROI marketing asset you own. A YDOP study of 34,111 roofing profiles found that 49% of roofing contractors have four or fewer reviews, 27% have no website link, and only 22% have fully optimized profiles. That gap is your opportunity.</p>
<p>Start with the basics. Set your primary category to "Roofing Contractor." Add secondary categories: "Siding Contractor," "Gutter Cleaning Service," and "Construction Company." Fill every field — description, service areas, business hours, appointment link, and attributes. Companies with complete profiles are 70% more likely to drive physical site visits and 50% more likely to convert searchers into buyers, according to Google's own data.</p>
<p>Upload at least 20 photos monthly. Before-and-after project shots, team photos, equipment images, and storm damage documentation all signal activity to Google's algorithm. Add a 15-second video testimonial. Profiles with video content see measurably higher engagement.</p>
<p>Post weekly updates using roofing-specific keywords. Write posts like "Free Hail Damage Inspection in Plano — This Week Only" or "GAF Master Elite Roof Replacement Completed in Frisco." Include your city names and ZIP codes naturally. These posts stay live for seven days and signal freshness to Google.</p>
<p>Enable messaging and respond within 15 minutes. A Dallas roofing firm that optimized response time from 4+ hours to under 15 minutes saw conversion rates jump from 3.2% to 18.7% during storm season. Set up an auto-reply for after-hours inquiries.</p>
<p>Target 50+ reviews with a 4.5+ star average. Roofing contractors with 50+ reviews and strong ratings have a 57% higher probability of ranking in the top three map positions, according to SEO Expert Pro's 2026 analysis. Send review requests via SMS within 24 hours of job completion — SMS review requests generate a 68% response rate versus 42% for email.</p>
<h4>Roofing Example</h4>
<p>A Michigan roofing contractor with three locations rebuilt their GBP strategy from scratch. They optimized all three listings, fixed NAP consistency, added regular posts, and implemented a photo upload schedule. Within six months, organic traffic grew 188% and GBP leads increased 145% — from roughly 400 monthly calls to 988 monthly calls.</p>
<hr />
<h3>Strategy 2: Build Roofing-Specific Location Pages</h3>
<p><strong>Best Season:</strong> Always-On<br />
<strong>Expected Lead Volume:</strong> 5–15 additional organic leads per month per page<br />
<strong>Cost to Implement:</strong> $300–$800 per page (one-time)</p>
<h4>How to Implement</h4>
<p>Generic "Areas We Serve" pages with a bullet list of cities do not rank. Google rewards depth. You need a dedicated page for every city and major neighborhood you target, written specifically for roofing searchers in that area.</p>
<p>Each location page needs unique content. Start with 800–1,200 words covering: the roofing challenges specific to that area (hail frequency, wind zones, building codes), types of roofs common in that neighborhood, local permit requirements, and recent projects you have completed there. Mention specific subdivisions, landmarks, and local weather patterns.</p>
<p>Structure each page with clear on-page SEO. Use the H1 format: "Roofing Contractor in [City], [State]." Include the city name in your H2s, meta title, meta description, and image alt text. Add a Google Map embed showing your service radius. Include a local phone number and a contact form pre-tagged with that location.</p>
<p>Add social proof specific to that city. Display reviews from customers in that area. Show before-and-after photos from projects in that ZIP code. Mention local certifications — "GAF Certified in [City]" or "Owens Corning Preferred Contractor serving [Neighborhood]."</p>
<p>Build internal links between location pages and your main service pages. Link from your "Roof Replacement" page to "Roof Replacement in Dallas" and back. This creates topical clusters that Google understands.</p>
<p>Do not duplicate content across pages. If you serve 20 cities, write 20 unique introductions. The rest can share a template structure, but the opening paragraphs, local references, and project examples must be original. Duplicate content triggers Google's thin content filter and tanks rankings.</p>
<h4>Roofing Example</h4>
<p>A Pennsylvania roofing company built location pages for 10 cities in their service area. Each page targeted "roofing [city]" and "roof repair [city]." Within 90 days, they ranked #1 for "roofing West Chester PA" and top three for nine additional cities. Organic clicks increased from 117 to 435 per month — a 3x improvement with zero ad spend.</p>
<hr />
<h3>Strategy 3: Create Storm Damage Landing Pages</h3>
<p><strong>Best Season:</strong> Always-On (peak activation in Spring/Summer)<br />
<strong>Expected Lead Volume:</strong> 20–60 storm leads per event<br />
<strong>Cost to Implement:</strong> $500–$1,200 per landing page (one-time)</p>
<h4>How to Implement</h4>
<p>Storm damage landing pages are your highest-converting digital asset during hail and wind events. Search volume for storm-related roofing terms spikes 300–800% within 48 hours of a severe weather event. Contractors with pre-built pages capture that surge. Contractors without them miss the window entirely.</p>
<p>Build three core landing pages: "[City] Hail Damage Roof Repair," "[City] Wind Damage Roof Repair," and "Roof Insurance Claim Assistance in [City]." Each page needs a mobile-first design with click-to-call buttons above the fold, a short form (three fields max: name, phone, address), and urgency-driven copy.</p>
<p>Write the headline for the homeowner in crisis. "Did Last Night's Storm Damage Your Roof? Free Inspection Today." Follow with a subheadline that removes friction: "We work directly with your insurance adjuster. You pick the shingle color. We handle everything else."</p>
<p>Include an FAQ section with schema markup. Answer questions like "What size hail damages a roof?" (1 inch or larger), "Will my insurance cover roof replacement?" (usually, if damage exceeds your deductible), and "How long do I have to file a claim?" (varies by state, typically 1–2 years). FAQ schema increases your chance of appearing in featured snippets and AI Overviews.</p>
<p>Add trust signals specific to storm work. Display your insurance license, GAF or Owens Corning certifications, BBB rating, and photos from previous storm restoration projects. Include a video walkthrough of your storm damage inspection process.</p>
<p>Set up the page for instant activation. Keep the page live year-round but pause the ads. When a storm hits, activate Google Ads and Local Services Ads pointing to this page within two hours. Pre-write three ad variations for each page so you are not writing copy during a crisis.</p>
<h4>Roofing Example</h4>
<p>A Dallas-Fort Worth roofing company built storm damage landing pages for five major cities before hail season. When a March 2025 hailstorm hit Plano with 2.5-inch hail, they activated pre-built campaigns within 90 minutes. Their "Plano Hail Damage Roof Repair" page generated 47 inspection requests in the first 48 hours. They closed 19 jobs from that single event with an average job value of $14,200.</p>
<hr />
<h3>Strategy 4: Develop an Insurance Claim Content Hub</h3>
<p><strong>Best Season:</strong> Always-On<br />
<strong>Expected Lead Volume:</strong> 3–8 organic leads per month from insurance-related searches<br />
<strong>Cost to Implement:</strong> $2,000–$5,000 (content creation, one-time)</p>
<h4>How to Implement</h4>
<p>Insurance claim content is the most underutilized SEO opportunity in roofing. Homeowners search "how to file a roof insurance claim" and "what does roof insurance cover" thousands of times per month in storm-prone markets. Most roofing companies ignore these queries. The ones who answer them build trust before the homeowner ever calls.</p>
<p>Create a dedicated "Insurance Claims" section on your website with five core pages: "How to File a Roof Insurance Claim," "What Homeowners Insurance Covers for Roofs," "Roof Insurance Claim Denied: What to Do Next," "How Roof Depreciation Affects Your Payout," and "Working With a Public Adjuster for Roof Claims."</p>
<p>Write each page at 1,500+ words with step-by-step instructions. Use plain language. Homeowners do not understand ACV versus RCV, depreciation schedules, or supplement filing. Explain these concepts with examples. "If your roof is 10 years old and your policy pays ACV, you might receive $6,000 for a $12,000 replacement. We can help you understand your actual coverage."</p>
<p>Add downloadable resources. Create a PDF checklist: "10 Things to Document Before the Adjuster Arrives." Gate it behind a simple form to capture emails. This builds your nurture list with homeowners actively dealing with roof damage.</p>
<p>Include video content. A 3-minute video walkthrough of an actual insurance inspection — showing what adjusters look for, how to document damage, and common mistakes — builds massive credibility. Upload it to YouTube with local keywords in the title and description.</p>
<p>Link this content hub to your storm damage landing pages and your main service pages. Internal linking strengthens topical authority and helps Google understand that your site is the definitive resource for roofing insurance questions in your market.</p>
<h4>Roofing Example</h4>
<p>A Colorado roofing company created a 10-article insurance claims content hub targeting hail-prone Denver suburbs. Within eight months, they ranked on page one for "how to file roof insurance claim Denver" and related terms. The hub generated 6–8 qualified leads per month from homeowners already in the claims process. Their close rate on these leads was 52% — nearly double their general organic close rate — because the prospects arrived pre-educated and pre-trusted.</p>
<hr />
<h3>Strategy 5: Build a Structured Referral Program</h3>
<p><strong>Best Season:</strong> Always-On<br />
<strong>Expected Lead Volume:</strong> 2–5 referral leads per month<br />
<strong>Cost to Implement:</strong> $100–$500/month (rewards + software)</p>
<h4>How to Implement</h4>
<p>Referral leads convert at 40–60% — the highest close rate of any lead source. Referred customers also have a 16% higher lifetime value than non-referred customers, according to referral marketing research. Yet most roofing companies treat referrals as an afterthought. A structured program turns happy customers into a predictable lead channel.</p>
<p>Design a two-sided incentive. The referrer gets something immediately valuable — not a discount on future roofing work they will not need for 15 years. Offer a $150–$250 gift card to a local home improvement store, restaurant, or Visa gift card. The referred homeowner gets a discount on their project — 5% off or a free gutter cleaning with roof replacement.</p>
<p>Auto-enroll every customer. Do not make people sign up. When you complete a job, the customer is in the program. Send their unique referral link via text within 24 hours of project completion. The message should read: "Your roof is complete. Love our work? Share this link with a neighbor and get a $200 gift card when they book. They will save 5% too."</p>
<p>Track everything in your CRM. Tag leads by source. Know which customers refer the most. Send quarterly thank-you notes to your top five referrers. Some roofing companies host an annual dinner for their best referral partners — a $500 investment that generates tens of thousands in repeat referrals.</p>
<p>Promote the program everywhere. Add a referral page to your website. Include program details on every invoice and receipt. Train your crew to mention it during the final walkthrough: "If you know anyone who needs a roof, we will give you $200 for every person you send our way."</p>
<p>Set a realistic benchmark. The global average referral rate is 2.35%. Aim for 2–3% of your jobs to come from referrals. If you complete 100 jobs per year, that is 2–3 referral jobs monthly. At $12,000 average job value, that is $288,000–$432,000 in annual revenue from a program that costs you $3,000–$6,000 in rewards.</p>
<h4>Roofing Example</h4>
<p>A Florida roofing company implemented an automated referral program through their CRM. Every customer received a text with a unique referral link 24 hours after job completion. They offered a $200 Visa gift card for every referred job that closed. Within 12 months, referrals grew from 4% to 11% of total jobs. Their customer acquisition cost for referral leads was 1.4% of job value versus 4–8% for Google Ads leads.</p>
<hr />
<h3>Strategy 6: Install an Interactive Roofing Calculator</h3>
<p><strong>Best Season:</strong> Always-On<br />
<strong>Expected Lead Volume:</strong> 10–30 qualified leads per month<br />
<strong>Cost to Implement:</strong> $200–$500/month (software) or $3,000–$8,000 (custom build)</p>
<h4>How to Implement</h4>
<p>Homeowners want to know what a new roof costs before they call. An interactive roofing calculator captures that demand and converts anonymous website visitors into named leads. Companies with instant estimate tools see significantly higher website conversion rates than those with static "Contact Us" forms.</p>
<p>Choose your calculator approach. For most roofers, a plug-and-play tool like Roofr, PSAI, or GeoQuote makes sense. These tools use satellite imagery to auto-measure roof square footage and deliver ballpark estimates based on your pricing. Custom-built calculators offer more control but require upfront development investment.</p>
<p>Configure the calculator with your real pricing. Input per-square costs for asphalt shingles, metal roofing, tile, and flat roof systems. Add regional modifiers for labor costs. Include financing options — showing a $15,000 roof as "$195/month" removes sticker shock and increases form completion.</p>
<p>Gate the full estimate behind minimal contact info. Ask for address, name, phone, and email. Do not require more than four fields. The address field is critical — it enables satellite measurement and qualifies the lead by service area.</p>
<p>Set up automated follow-up. The calculator should trigger an SMS with the estimate within 60 seconds, followed by a call from your sales team within 5 minutes. Leads contacted within 5 minutes are 9x more likely to convert than leads contacted after 30 minutes.</p>
<p>Place the calculator prominently. Add it to your homepage above the fold. Embed it on every service page. Create a dedicated "Get an Instant Roof Estimate" page optimized for "roof cost calculator [city]" searches. Promote it via social media and direct mail with QR codes.</p>
<h4>Roofing Example</h4>
<p>A Texas roofing company added an instant estimate widget to their homepage and promoted it through Facebook ads targeting homeowners in ZIP codes with roofs older than 15 years. The calculator generated 23 qualified leads in the first month at an effective cost per lead of $18. Their static contact form, by comparison, generated 4 leads that same month. The calculator leads also closed at a 28% rate versus 12% for general website inquiries because they arrived with pricing expectations already set.</p>
<hr />
<h3>Strategy 7: Implement an Automated Review Generation System</h3>
<p><strong>Best Season:</strong> Always-On<br />
<strong>Expected Lead Volume:</strong> Indirect — improves conversion on all channels by 15–30%<br />
<strong>Cost to Implement:</strong> $50–$300/month (software)</p>
<h4>How to Implement</h4>
<p>Reviews are not a vanity metric. They are a ranking factor, a conversion multiplier, and a competitive moat. A YDOP study found that 83% of homeowners trust online reviews as much as personal recommendations when choosing a roofer. A single one-star review drop can decrease lead-to-job conversion by 14%. Meanwhile, roofing contractors with 50+ reviews and 4.5+ stars have a 57% higher chance of ranking in the top three map positions.</p>
<p>Automate the entire process. Connect your CRM (JobNimbus, AccuLynx, ServiceTitan) to a review platform (NiceJob, Birdeye, or your CRM's built-in tool). When a job is marked complete, trigger an SMS review request within 2–24 hours. SMS review requests achieve a 68% response rate versus 42% for email.</p>
<p>Use sentiment pre-screening. Send a two-step request: first ask "How would you rate your experience? 1–5 stars." If they select 4–5 stars, redirect them to Google. If they select 1–3 stars, redirect them to a private feedback form. This intercepts negative reviews before they hit your public profile.</p>
<p>Follow up automatically. If the customer does not leave a review within 48 hours, send a second SMS. If no response after 96 hours, send a final email. Data shows that 40% of reviews come from follow-up touches, not the initial request.</p>
<p>Respond to every review within 24 hours. For positive reviews, thank the customer by name and mention something specific about their project. For negative reviews, apologize publicly, offer to make it right, and move the conversation offline. Google rewards active review management with higher local rankings.</p>
<p>Set targets by volume. A roofer completing 8 jobs per week should aim for 30–45 net new reviews per quarter. At that pace, you will move from page-two map pack obscurity into the top-three local pack within six months.</p>
<h4>Roofing Example</h4>
<p>Cathedral Roofing in Florida implemented an automated SMS review system through their CRM. They sent review requests within four hours of job completion and followed up once at 48 hours. In seven months, they added 120+ Google reviews, tripled their monthly review volume, increased referral leads by 22%, and raised their lead-to-sale conversion rate by 18%. Their Google rating improved from 4.2 to 4.8 stars.</p>
<hr />
<h3>Strategy 8: Build a Commercial Roofing Portfolio</h3>
<p><strong>Best Season:</strong> Always-On<br />
<strong>Expected Lead Volume:</strong> 2–6 commercial leads per month<br />
<strong>Cost to Implement:</strong> $1,500–$4,000 (photography, website development)</p>
<h4>How to Implement</h4>
<p>Commercial roofing is the fastest-growing segment of the U.S. roofing market, expanding at 8.05% CAGR, according to Mordor Intelligence's 2025 analysis. Data centers, warehouses, and healthcare facilities are driving massive demand for low-slope roof systems. Yet most roofing companies market to commercial prospects the same way they market to homeowners — with generic service pages and residential imagery.</p>
<p>Build a separate commercial section on your website. Create dedicated pages for each system you install: TPO, EPDM, PVC, modified bitumen, metal, and roof coatings. Each page should include typical applications, lifespan expectations, energy efficiency data, and warranty information.</p>
<p>Develop detailed case studies. Commercial buyers need specifics. For each major project, document: building type, square footage, existing roof condition, system installed, project duration, challenges overcome, and total cost. Include before-and-after photos, drone footage, and a testimonial from the facility manager or property owner.</p>
<p>Add commercial-specific trust signals. Display your safety record (OSHA compliance, EMR rating), bonding capacity, manufacturer certifications (Carlisle, GAF Commercial, Johns Manville), and project size range. Commercial buyers care about liability insurance limits and workers' compensation coverage — mention these explicitly.</p>
<p>Target commercial keywords. Optimize for "commercial roofing contractor [city]," "flat roof repair [city]," "TPO roofing [city]," and "roof maintenance program [city]." These have lower search volume than residential terms but significantly less competition and higher job values.</p>
<p>Build a LinkedIn presence. Commercial roofing is B2B. Create a company page, share project updates, and connect with facility managers, property managers, and commercial real estate brokers. Post weekly content: project photos, roofing maintenance tips, and industry news.</p>
<h4>Roofing Example</h4>
<p>12 Stones Roofing in Pasadena, Texas built a commercial roofing portfolio page and optimized for "commercial roofing Pasadena." Within four months, they received a call from a property manager at a local business park. The call turned into a 26-roof maintenance and replacement contract — the largest single deal in company history. The entire contract was generated from one organic search during their slow season.</p>
<hr />
<h2>Spring Strategies (March–May)</h2>
<p>Spring is when homeowners emerge from winter and start noticing problems. It is also hail season in much of the country. These three strategies capture the spring mindset.</p>
<hr />
<h3>Strategy 9: Launch Hail Damage Awareness Campaigns</h3>
<p><strong>Best Season:</strong> Spring<br />
<strong>Expected Lead Volume:</strong> 15–35 leads per campaign<br />
<strong>Cost to Implement:</strong> $1,500–$5,000 (ads + content + mailers)</p>
<h4>How to Implement</h4>
<p>Spring hail season generates some of the highest-value leads in roofing. The U.S. saw 142 hail damage days in 2025, with over 600,000 homes hit by 2-inch-plus hail. Texas alone had 235,000+ homes affected. Homeowners who understand hail damage and know who to call book inspections at 2–3x the rate of general roofing inquiries.</p>
<p>Start with education, not sales. Create a "Hail Damage Awareness" landing page that teaches homeowners how to spot hail damage from the ground. Include photos of bruised shingles, dented vents, and cracked skylights. Explain the 1-inch hail threshold that most insurance companies use for coverage. Add a video walkthrough of a hail inspection.</p>
<p>Run Facebook and Instagram ads targeting homeowners in hail-prone ZIP codes. Use interest targeting: homeowners, home improvement, insurance. Geo-target neighborhoods with roofs older than 10 years. Ad creative should show actual hail damage photos with copy like "Did March Hail Damage Your Roof? 5 Signs to Check From Your Driveway."</p>
<p>Send direct mail to hail-prone areas before storm season. A postcard with a QR code linking to your hail damage guide positions you as the local expert. Mail in late February or early March — before the first storms hit.</p>
<p>Partner with local meteorologists or weather stations. Offer to be their "roofing expert" during storm season. A single TV segment mentioning your company name drives dozens of branded searches.</p>
<p>Activate immediately after any hail event. Have pre-written ad copy, landing pages, and email sequences ready to deploy within two hours. The first 72 hours after hail are critical — homeowners who get an inspection within this window book with that contractor 68–74% of the time.</p>
<h4>Roofing Example</h4>
<p>A roofing company in Oklahoma City launched a hail awareness campaign in February 2025. They mailed 5,000 postcards to neighborhoods with roofs aged 12+ years, ran Facebook ads targeting the same ZIP codes, and published a "Hail Damage Guide" blog post. When a March hailstorm hit with 1.75-inch hail, they activated pre-built campaigns within 90 minutes. The campaign generated 31 inspection requests in 72 hours. They closed 14 jobs with an average value of $13,800.</p>
<hr />
<h3>Strategy 10: Partner with Insurance Agents for Referrals</h3>
<p><strong>Best Season:</strong> Spring (peak storm season)<br />
<strong>Expected Lead Volume:</strong> 3–10 qualified leads per month per agent<br />
<strong>Cost to Implement:</strong> $500–$2,000/month (relationship building, co-marketing)</p>
<h4>How to Implement</h4>
<p>Insurance agents are the most underutilized referral source in roofing. When a homeowner calls their agent after storm damage, the agent needs a roofer they trust. Be that roofer. One strong agent relationship can generate 3–10 qualified leads per month during storm season.</p>
<p>Identify the right agents. Target independent insurance agents who write homeowners policies in your service area. Avoid captive agents (State Farm, Allstate exclusive) who have less flexibility in referrals. Use LinkedIn to find agents, or purchase a list from your local Chamber of Commerce.</p>
<p>Approach with value first. Do not ask for referrals on the first contact. Offer to host a free lunch-and-learn: "How to Spot Roof Damage: A Guide for Insurance Professionals." Bring photo examples of legitimate hail damage versus normal wear. Teach agents what to tell homeowners when they call with a potential claim.</p>
<p>Create co-branded materials. Develop a one-page handout the agent can give homeowners: "5 Steps to Take After Storm Damage" with your company name, phone number, and the agent's name. The agent looks helpful. You get pre-qualified leads who already trust you through the association.</p>
<p>Deliver on speed and documentation. When an agent refers a homeowner, inspect within 24 hours and provide a detailed report with photos, measurements, and an Xactimate estimate. Agents refer contractors who make their job easier, not harder.</p>
<p>Track and reward. Use a dedicated phone number or landing page for each agent so you can measure their referrals. Send a handwritten thank-you note after every referral. Consider a quarterly gift basket or charitable donation in the agent's name for top referrers.</p>
<h4>Roofing Example</h4>
<p>A roofing contractor in Denver identified 12 independent insurance agents in their market. They hosted monthly coffee meetings, provided free hail damage training, and created co-branded storm guides. Within 18 months, those 12 agents generated 147 qualified leads. The close rate on agent-referred leads was 44% — nearly double their general lead close rate of 23%.</p>
<hr />
<h3>Strategy 11: Create a Free Roof Inspection Offer</h3>
<p><strong>Best Season:</strong> Spring<br />
<strong>Expected Lead Volume:</strong> 20–50 inspection requests per month<br />
<strong>Cost to Implement:</strong> $300–$800/month (ads + landing page)</p>
<h4>How to Implement</h4>
<p>Free roof inspections are the single most effective lead magnet in roofing. They remove risk for the homeowner and give you a foot in the door. Inspection campaigns in spring convert 28% higher than in other seasons because homeowners are already thinking about winter damage and upcoming storms.</p>
<p>Build a dedicated landing page. The headline should be specific and urgent: "Free Roof Inspection in [City]: Book This Week." Include a short form (name, phone, address), a photo of a friendly inspector, and trust badges. Add an FAQ section answering "How long does an inspection take?" (30–45 minutes), "Will you go on my roof?" (yes, with your permission), and "Do I have to be home?" (yes, for access).</p>
<p>Run Google Local Services Ads for "free roof inspection [city]." LSAs show the Google Guaranteed badge and charge only for qualified calls. Cost per lead typically runs $65–$95 for inspection campaigns — significantly lower than replacement-keyword campaigns.</p>
<p>Promote through multiple channels simultaneously. Run Facebook ads to homeowners aged 35–65 in your service area. Send email campaigns to your past customer list. Add a banner to your website homepage. Train your CSRs to mention the offer on every inbound call.</p>
<p>Set inspection capacity limits. Create scarcity by limiting appointments to 20 per week. Update your landing page: "Only 3 Free Inspections Left This Week." Scarcity increases conversion rates by 15–25%.</p>
<p>Convert inspections into sales. The inspection is a sales appointment, not just an assessment. Train inspectors to document all findings, explain options, and present financing. The national average close rate from inspection to signed contract is 35–45% for trained sales teams.</p>
<h4>Roofing Example</h4>
<p>A roofing company in Atlanta ran a "Free Spring Roof Inspection" campaign during March and April. They promoted it through Google LSAs, Facebook ads, and email to 2,400 past customers. The campaign generated 87 inspection requests in eight weeks. Their sales team closed 34 jobs from those inspections — a 39% close rate — with an average job value of $11,400. Total revenue: $387,600 from a $4,200 marketing investment.</p>
<hr />
<h2>Summer Strategies (June–August)</h2>
<p>Summer brings peak storm activity in many regions and the highest search volume for emergency roofing services. These strategies capture urgency and build brand presence.</p>
<hr />
<h3>Strategy 12: Run Storm-Response PPC Campaigns</h3>
<p><strong>Best Season:</strong> Summer<br />
<strong>Expected Lead Volume:</strong> 30–100 leads per storm event<br />
<strong>Cost to Implement:</strong> $2,500–$8,000 per storm event</p>
<h4>How to Implement</h4>
<p>Storm-response PPC is the highest-intensity, highest-reward marketing tactic in roofing. A single severe hailstorm can spike roofing search volume by 300–800% within 48 hours. Contractors who activate campaigns within two hours capture 70% more storm leads than those who wait 24 hours.</p>
<p>Pre-build three paused campaign templates in Google Ads: "Hail Damage — [City]," "Wind Damage — [City]," and "Emergency Roof Repair — [City]." Write ad copy in advance. Example headlines: "Hail Damage Roof Repair [City] — Free Inspection Today" and "Emergency Roof Tarping Available Now — 24/7 Response."</p>
<p>Set a storm reserve budget. Pre-fund $2,500–$8,000 specifically for storm activation. Deploy 50% of the budget in the first 24 hours, 30% in hours 24–72, and 20% for the two-week tail. Post-storm CPCs spike 40–120% in the first 48 hours, so front-load your spending.</p>
<p>Use geo-targeting precision. Target only ZIP codes confirmed by NOAA or Hail Trace data as affected. Exclude renters and commercial properties unless you serve both. Focus on homeowners in neighborhoods with roofs aged 10+ years.</p>
<p>Build storm-specific landing pages. The page headline should reference the specific storm: "Roof Damage From the June 12 Hailstorm? We Are Inspecting [Neighborhood] Today." Include a real-time update: "Currently serving the [Subdivision] area. 12 inspections booked today."</p>
<p>Integrate with your CRM. Auto-tag storm leads by event, ZIP code, and hail size. Trigger automated SMS sequences: "Thanks for requesting a storm inspection. We will call you within 15 minutes. Reply STOP to opt out."</p>
<h4>Roofing Example</h4>
<p>A roofing company in Dallas pre-built storm campaigns and integrated Hail Trace data with their Google Ads account. When a June 2025 hailstorm produced 2.25-inch hail across northern Dallas suburbs, their campaigns activated automatically. They spent $5,200 over 10 days and generated 64 qualified leads. Their sales team closed 22 jobs with an average value of $15,200. Revenue: $334,400. ROI: 6,430%.</p>
<hr />
<h3>Strategy 13: Launch Commercial Roof Maintenance Campaigns</h3>
<p><strong>Best Season:</strong> Summer<br />
<strong>Expected Lead Volume:</strong> 5–15 commercial maintenance contracts per campaign<br />
<strong>Cost to Implement:</strong> $1,000–$3,000/month (LinkedIn + direct outreach)</p>
<h4>How to Implement</h4>
<p>Summer is when commercial buildings experience peak thermal stress. Flat roof systems expand and contract, seams open, and leaks appear. Facility managers are actively looking for maintenance providers during this season. A well-timed maintenance campaign generates recurring revenue that smooths your residential seasonal dips.</p>
<p>Build a "Roof Maintenance Program" landing page. Explain the program structure: bi-annual inspections, priority emergency response, documented condition reports, and discounted repairs. Price it at $0.10–$0.20 per square foot annually. For a 50,000-square-foot warehouse, that is $5,000–$10,000 in recurring annual revenue.</p>
<p>Target facility managers on LinkedIn. Use LinkedIn Sales Navigator to find facility managers, property managers, and directors of operations within 25 miles of your office. Filter by company size (50–500 employees) and industry (warehousing, manufacturing, healthcare, retail). Send connection requests with a value-first message: "Hi [Name], I help facility managers in [City] prevent emergency roof leaks. Would you be open to a free roof condition assessment?"</p>
<p>Create a direct mail piece for commercial prospects. Send a "Summer Roof Stress Report" with thermal imaging photos showing heat buildup on flat roofs. Include a QR code to book a free assessment. Mail to property management companies and industrial parks.</p>
<p>Develop a multi-touch nurture sequence. Send four emails over six weeks: (1) the maintenance program overview, (2) a case study from a similar building, (3) a roof condition checklist they can use internally, and (4) a limited-time offer for a free first inspection.</p>
<h4>Roofing Example</h4>
<p>A commercial roofing contractor in Cleveland launched a summer maintenance campaign targeting 200 facility managers within 30 miles. They sent LinkedIn messages, mailed thermal imaging reports, and followed up with email nurture sequences. The campaign generated 12 roof assessments, which converted into 7 annual maintenance contracts and 2 full replacements. The maintenance contracts alone added $47,000 in recurring annual revenue.</p>
<hr />
<h3>Strategy 14: Sponsor Community Events and Sports Teams</h3>
<p><strong>Best Season:</strong> Summer<br />
<strong>Expected Lead Volume:</strong> 1–3 direct leads per month plus significant brand awareness<br />
<strong>Cost to Implement:</strong> $500–$2,500 per sponsorship</p>
<h4>How to Implement</h4>
<p>Community sponsorships generate leads indirectly through brand recognition, local backlinks, and word-of-mouth. When a homeowner needs a roofer, they call the company whose name they have seen on Little League jerseys and church bulletins. Sponsorships also earn genuine local backlinks that improve your Google rankings.</p>
<p>Choose sponsorships strategically. Prioritize events and organizations that reach homeowners in your target neighborhoods: youth sports leagues, school booster clubs, church festivals, and neighborhood association events. Avoid sponsorships that reach broad, untargeted audiences.</p>
<p>Negotiate visibility. Ensure your company name appears on banners, programs, team jerseys, and announcement boards. Get a link from the organization's website to yours — this is often overlooked but provides real local SEO value. A Chamber of Commerce backlink or well-established youth sports league link passes geographic relevance signals to Google.</p>
<p>Activate the sponsorship digitally. Post photos from the event on your Google Business Profile, Facebook, and Instagram. Tag the organization and attendees. Write a blog post: "[Company Name] Sponsors [Event] — Supporting [City] Families." This turns a $1,000 sponsorship into content that keeps working.</p>
<p>Track attribution. Use a dedicated phone number or landing page for each major sponsorship. Ask every new lead: "How did you hear about us?" Calculate break-even: one residential roof replacement ($12,000) typically covers a full year of community sponsorships.</p>
<h4>Roofing Example</h4>
<p>A roofing company in suburban Chicago sponsored four youth baseball teams ($800 total), the local high school drama program ($500), and their Chamber of Commerce ($400). They received backlinks from three of the organizations' websites. Within six months, organic rankings improved for four city-specific keywords. They also received five direct calls attributed to sponsorships, closing three jobs worth $38,500 total. Their $1,700 investment generated a 2,165% return.</p>
<hr />
<h2>Fall Strategies (September–November)</h2>
<p>Fall is the last chance to book work before winter. Homeowners feel urgency. Insurance deductibles reset in January. These strategies capitalize on the pre-winter mindset.</p>
<hr />
<h3>Strategy 15: Deploy Pre-Winter Roof Inspection Campaigns</h3>
<p><strong>Best Season:</strong> Fall<br />
<strong>Expected Lead Volume:</strong> 15–40 inspection requests per month<br />
<strong>Cost to Implement:</strong> $1,000–$3,000/month (ads + email + direct mail)</p>
<h4>How to Implement</h4>
<p>Fall produces higher conversion rates than any other season. Homeowners know winter is coming. They have seen the forecasts. The urgency is real but not immediate — they have time to research and decide, which means they actually follow through on inspections.</p>
<p>Create a "Winter-Ready Roof" campaign. The messaging should tap into homeowner anxiety: "Is Your Roof Ready for Winter?" and "Last Chance for Roof Repairs Before Freezing Temperatures." Use imagery of ice dams, snow buildup, and cracked shingles.</p>
<p>Run Google Ads for pre-winter keywords. Target "roof inspection before winter," "winter roof repair [city]," and "ice dam prevention [city]." These keywords have lower competition than spring storm terms and convert at 20–30% higher rates because the intent is proactive, not panic-driven.</p>
<p>Email your past customer list with a seasonal reminder. Segment by roof age: customers with roofs 8–15 years old get the "pre-winter inspection" message. Customers with new roofs (0–3 years) get a "gutter cleaning and maintenance" offer. Segmented campaigns outperform generic blasts by 20–50%.</p>
<p>Offer a "Fall Home Readiness Bundle." Partner with an HVAC company and a gutter cleaning service. The homeowner gets a roof inspection, furnace tune-up, and gutter cleaning for a package price. You split the revenue and share the leads. Cross-promotion expands your reach without additional ad spend.</p>
<p>Create urgency with scheduling limits. "We only have 40 pre-winter inspection slots available. Book by October 15 to guarantee service before the first freeze." Limited availability increases booking rates by 15–25%.</p>
<h4>Roofing Example</h4>
<p>A roofing company in Minneapolis ran a "Winter-Ready Roof" campaign every September and October. They promoted it through Google Ads, email to 3,100 past customers, and Facebook ads targeting homeowners aged 40+. In fall 2024, they booked 62 inspections and closed 27 jobs worth $312,000. Their fall close rate was 43% — 8 percentage points higher than their spring average.</p>
<hr />
<h3>Strategy 16: Cross-Sell Gutter and Siding Services</h3>
<p><strong>Best Season:</strong> Fall<br />
<strong>Expected Lead Volume:</strong> 5–15 additional service jobs per month<br />
<strong>Cost to Implement:</strong> $200–$600/month (email + ads)</p>
<h4>How to Implement</h4>
<p>Your existing customer base is your cheapest source of new revenue. A homeowner who trusts you with their roof will trust you with gutters, siding, and insulation. Cross-selling increases customer lifetime value and smooths revenue during shoulder season.</p>
<p>Identify cross-sell opportunities in your CRM. Flag customers who received roof work in the past 24 months but have not purchased gutters, siding, or attic insulation. Segment by service needed: "gutter replacement," "siding repair," or "attic insulation upgrade."</p>
<p>Create bundled offers. "Book gutter cleaning with your fall roof inspection and save 20%." Or "Replace your siding this fall and get free gutter guards." Bundles increase average transaction value by 25–40%.</p>
<p>Use before-and-after photos in your marketing. Show a house with old gutters and faded siding next to the same house with new gutters and fresh siding. Visual transformation sells better than descriptions.</p>
<p>Train your inspectors to identify cross-sell needs during every roof inspection. If they see clogged gutters, damaged siding, or inadequate insulation, they should document it and mention it to the homeowner. A simple observation — "Your gutters are pulling away from the fascia, which could cause water damage to your new roof" — generates add-on sales.</p>
<p>Send a dedicated cross-sell email sequence. Email 1: "Your roof is protected. What about your gutters?" Email 2: Before-and-after photos of a gutter replacement. Email 3: Limited-time offer ending October 31. Email 4: Final reminder with scarcity messaging.</p>
<h4>Roofing Example</h4>
<p>A roofing contractor in Portland added gutter and siding services and promoted them to their existing customer base. They sent a three-email fall sequence to 1,800 past customers who had roof work but no gutter or siding services. The campaign generated 23 gutter jobs and 8 siding jobs. Average gutter job: $2,400. Average siding job: $18,500. Total additional revenue: $203,200 with zero ad spend on new customer acquisition.</p>
<hr />
<h3>Strategy 17: Publish Storm Preparedness Content</h3>
<p><strong>Best Season:</strong> Fall<br />
<strong>Expected Lead Volume:</strong> 5–12 organic leads per month<br />
<strong>Cost to Implement:</strong> $800–$2,000 (content creation, one-time)</p>
<h4>How to Implement</h4>
<p>Fall is when homeowners research storm preparation. They search "how to prepare roof for hurricane," "winter storm roof protection," and "how to prevent ice dams." Content that answers these questions ranks in fall, gets shared on social media, and builds authority that pays off during the next storm season.</p>
<p>Create a "Storm Preparedness Guide" as a comprehensive blog post or downloadable PDF. Cover: how to inspect your roof before storm season, what supplies to keep on hand (tarps, plywood, contractor contact info), how to document your roof's condition for insurance, and what to do immediately after a storm. Target 2,500+ words.</p>
<p>Build individual articles for specific questions. "How to Prevent Ice Dams: A Homeowner's Guide" targets northern markets. "Hurricane Roof Preparation Checklist" targets coastal markets. "How to Spot Wind Damage After a Storm" works everywhere. Each article should include step-by-step instructions, photos, and a downloadable checklist.</p>
<p>Optimize for featured snippets. Use question-based H2s: "What supplies do I need before a storm?" Answer in 40–60 words immediately after the H2. Use numbered lists for step-by-step processes. Google pulls this content directly into featured snippets and AI Overviews.</p>
<p>Promote the content through multiple channels. Share on Facebook and Nextdoor in September and October. Email it to your customer list. Pitch it to local news stations as a "storm season prep" resource. A single TV mention drives hundreds of visits.</p>
<p>Gate the full PDF behind an email capture. "Download our free 12-Point Storm Preparedness Checklist." This builds your email list with homeowners actively thinking about roof protection — the highest-intent audience for future campaigns.</p>
<h4>Roofing Example</h4>
<p>A roofing company in Tampa published a "Hurricane Roof Preparation Guide" in August 2024. The 3,200-word article included a downloadable checklist, video embed, and FAQ schema. Within three months, it ranked #1 for "how to prepare roof for hurricane Tampa" and generated 8–12 organic leads per month during storm season. The article also earned backlinks from two local news stations and a regional insurance blog, strengthening their overall domain authority.</p>
<hr />
<h2>Winter Strategies (December–February)</h2>
<p>Winter is slow for most roofers. But the contractors who use winter strategically — building systems, creating content, and nurturing relationships — start spring with a full pipeline while competitors scramble.</p>
<hr />
<h3>Strategy 18: Partner with Interior Restoration Companies</h3>
<p><strong>Best Season:</strong> Winter<br />
<strong>Expected Lead Volume:</strong> 3–8 referral leads per month<br />
<strong>Cost to Implement:</strong> $0–$500/month (relationship building)</p>
<h4>How to Implement</h4>
<p>Winter roof leaks create interior damage. Water mitigation companies like ServPro and ServiceMaster get the first call. They dry the house, then the homeowner needs a roofer. If you are the roofer they recommend, you get pre-qualified leads with urgent needs.</p>
<p>Identify water mitigation and restoration companies in your market. Search "water damage restoration [city]" and "mold remediation [city]." Target mid-sized companies (5–20 employees) that handle residential work. Large national franchises often have preferred vendor lists that are harder to break into.</p>
<p>Approach with a mutual value proposition. Offer to refer homeowners with water damage to them. In exchange, ask them to refer homeowners who need roof repairs. This is a true partnership, not a one-sided ask.</p>
<p>Create a referral kit. Include your business cards, a one-page service overview, photos of your work, and a QR code linking to your website. Drop it off in person. Face-to-face introductions build trust faster than emails.</p>
<p>Offer co-branded materials. Create a handout both companies can give homeowners: "What to Do After a Roof Leak: A Guide from [Restoration Co.] and [Your Roofing Co.]." Each company gets exposure to the other's customer base.</p>
<p>Set expectations and follow up. Meet monthly for coffee. Share lead tracking so both sides see the value. Send a thank-you gift card for every referral that closes. Restoration companies refer contractors who make them look good — be responsive, professional, and easy to work with.</p>
<h4>Roofing Example</h4>
<p>A roofing contractor in Denver partnered with three local water mitigation companies. He provided them with referral cards and promised 24-hour response times for their mutual customers. In the first winter, the partnerships generated 19 leads. He closed 11 jobs worth $142,000. The restoration companies appreciated his speed so much that two of them added him to their "preferred contractor" lists, generating year-round referrals.</p>
<hr />
<h3>Strategy 19: Prepare Commercial Roofing Bids for Spring</h3>
<p><strong>Best Season:</strong> Winter<br />
<strong>Expected Lead Volume:</strong> 2–5 commercial proposals per month<br />
<strong>Cost to Implement:</strong> $500–$1,500/month (prospecting + proposal development)</p>
<h4>How to Implement</h4>
<p>Commercial roofing decisions happen in winter for spring execution. Facility managers finalize budgets in Q4 and Q1. Property management companies issue RFPs in January and February. If you start prospecting in December, you are ahead of contractors who wait for spring.</p>
<p>Build a target list of commercial properties. Use CoStar, LoopNet, or your local property assessor database to identify buildings with roofs approaching end-of-life. Target properties 15–25 years old with flat or low-slope roofs. Filter by ownership type: REITs, local property management companies, and industrial owner-operators.</p>
<p>Create a commercial prospecting cadence. Week 1: LinkedIn connection request to the facility manager. Week 2: Follow-up message offering a free roof condition report. Week 3: Cold email with a case study from a similar building. Week 4: Phone call. Week 6: Direct mail piece with a thermal imaging photo. Most commercial deals require 6–12 touchpoints over 3–6 months.</p>
<p>Develop proposal templates for common project types. Create a TPO replacement template, a coating restoration template, and a maintenance program template. Pre-populate them with your standard pricing, warranty terms, and project timelines. This reduces proposal turnaround from days to hours.</p>
<p>Register for government bidding platforms. Create accounts on SAM.gov (federal), BidNet, DemandStar, and your state's procurement portal. Federal agencies allocate nine-figure roofing budgets annually. A single government contract can be worth $500,000–$2,000,000.</p>
<h4>Roofing Example</h4>
<p>A commercial roofing contractor in Phoenix used winter to build a prospect list of 150 industrial buildings aged 18–25 years. They sent LinkedIn messages, cold emails, and direct mail pieces from December through February. By March, they had submitted 11 proposals. Four converted into projects worth $890,000 total. The contractor attributed the success to starting prospecting in December while competitors waited for spring.</p>
<hr />
<h3>Strategy 20: Invest in Off-Season Content Creation</h3>
<p><strong>Best Season:</strong> Winter<br />
<strong>Expected Lead Volume:</strong> 10–25 organic leads per month (starting in spring)<br />
<strong>Cost to Implement:</strong> $1,500–$4,000/month (content creation)</p>
<h4>How to Implement</h4>
<p>SEO is a lagging strategy. Content you publish in January ranks in March. Content you publish in March ranks in May. Winter is when you create the content that generates leads in spring and summer. Contractors who batch-create content in winter start the busy season with a full pipeline of organic leads.</p>
<p>Audit your existing content. Use Google Search Console to identify pages ranking on page two (positions 11–20). These are your quickest wins — small improvements can push them to page one. Also identify content gaps: topics your competitors cover that you do not.</p>
<p>Create a 90-day content calendar. Plan 12 articles covering: spring storm prep, hail damage guides for your specific region, insurance claim processes, roofing material comparisons, cost guides for your city, and commercial roofing topics. Assign each article to a writer or write them yourself during slow days.</p>
<p>Write location-specific cost guides. "How Much Does a Roof Replacement Cost in [City]?" is one of the highest-intent queries in roofing. These guides should be 2,000+ words with real pricing data, material comparisons, and financing options. Update them annually.</p>
<p>Build FAQ schema into every article. Add 5–10 FAQ sections to each piece. This increases your chances of appearing in featured snippets and AI Overviews. A single featured snippet can drive 20–40% of the clicks for a high-volume query.</p>
<p>Repurpose content across formats. Turn a blog post into a video script, an infographic, a social media carousel, and an email newsletter. One piece of core content should produce five derivative assets. This maximizes your investment.</p>
<h4>Roofing Example</h4>
<p>A roofing company in Kansas City created 16 articles during December and January — a mix of storm guides, cost articles, and insurance content. They published two articles per week starting in February. By April, 8 of the 16 articles ranked on page one for their target keywords. Organic traffic increased from 800 to 2,400 monthly visitors. The content generated 14–18 organic leads per month by May, with an effective cost per lead of $12 (amortized over 12 months).</p>
<hr />
<h3>Strategy 21: Launch a Winter Referral Incentive Push</h3>
<p><strong>Best Season:</strong> Winter<br />
<strong>Expected Lead Volume:</strong> 3–8 referral leads per month<br />
<strong>Cost to Implement:</strong> $300–$800/month (enhanced rewards)</p>
<h4>How to Implement</h4>
<p>Winter is the perfect time to activate your referral program because your past customers are not hearing from other roofers. While competitors go silent, you stay top-of-mind with a targeted referral campaign.</p>
<p>Enhance your standard referral offer for winter. If you normally offer $200 for referrals, increase it to $350 for December through February. Add urgency: "Double Your Referral Reward — $350 for Every Friend You Send Our Way. Ends February 28." Higher incentives during slow season cost less than acquiring leads through ads.</p>
<p>Send a dedicated referral email to your entire customer base. Subject line: "We Need Your Help This Winter." Body: "Winter is our slow season, and we would rather work on your neighbor's roof than lay off our crew. If you know anyone who needs roofing work, we will give you $350 when they book. Here is your personal referral link."</p>
<p>Add a physical referral card to every winter invoice. A simple card that says "Give this to a friend. They save $250 on their roof. You get $350." Physical reminders outperform digital ones because they sit on the kitchen counter, not in an inbox.</p>
<p>Create a "Neighbor Discount" program. If you complete a job in a neighborhood, mail a postcard to the 20 closest homes: "We just replaced [Neighbor's Name]'s roof on [Street]. Book an inspection this month and save $200." Social proof from a known neighbor increases response rates by 30–50%.</p>
<p>Track and celebrate publicly. Post on social media: "Congratulations to the Johnson family — they referred two neighbors and earned $700 in referral rewards this month!" Public recognition encourages others to participate.</p>
<h4>Roofing Example</h4>
<p>A roofing company in Ohio ran a "Winter Referral Boost" campaign from January through February. They increased their referral reward from $200 to $350, sent two emails to their 1,400-customer database, and mailed referral cards with every invoice. The campaign generated 14 referral leads. They closed 6 jobs worth $71,000. Total referral reward cost: $2,100. ROI: 3,280%.</p>
