<h2>FAQ</h2>
<h3>What is the best marketing strategy for roofing companies?</h3>
<p>The best strategy depends on your market and season, but Google Business Profile optimization delivers the highest consistent ROI. A fully optimized GBP generates 15–40 qualified calls per month at minimal cost. For storm-prone markets, storm-response PPC campaigns generate the highest volume during events. For year-round stability, a combination of GBP optimization, SEO content, and a structured referral program creates predictable lead flow.</p>
<h3>How much should a roofing company spend on marketing?</h3>
<p>Most successful roofing companies spend 5–10% of revenue on marketing. For a $2M company, that is $100,000–$200,000 annually. Allocate 60% to digital (Google Ads, SEO, social media) and 40% to traditional and relationship marketing (referrals, community events, insurance partnerships). New companies should spend more (10–15%) to build awareness. Established companies with strong organic presence can spend less (4–7%).</p>
<h3>What is a good cost per lead for roofing?</h3>
<p>Cost per lead varies dramatically by channel. Google Search Ads average $80–$350 per lead. Google Local Services Ads range $25–$150. SEO leads cost $10–$50 when amortized over time. Referral leads cost $100–$500 in rewards. Shared lead platforms (Angi, HomeAdvisor) charge $15–$90 per lead but sell them to 4–10 contractors simultaneously. The "best" cost per lead depends on your close rate and average job value. A $200 lead that closes at 30% on a $14,000 job is highly profitable.</p>
<h3>How do roofing companies get leads from insurance companies?</h3>
<p>Build relationships with independent insurance agents and adjusters. Offer free educational workshops. Provide fast, documented estimates with Xactimate breakdowns. Enroll in carrier preferred vendor programs (State Farm Select Service, Allstate Good Hands Network). Create insurance-specific content on your website. Be present at adjuster inspections. Always verify state regulations — some states prohibit referral fees or gifts to adjusters.</p>
<h3>When is the best time to market a roofing company?</h3>
<p>Market year-round, but adjust your message by season. Spring: hail damage awareness and free inspections. Summer: storm response and commercial maintenance. Fall: pre-winter inspections and cross-selling. Winter: referral campaigns, content creation, and commercial bid preparation. Companies that market year-round significantly outperform seasonal-only advertisers.</p>
<h3>How long does SEO take to work for roofing companies?</h3>
<p>SEO typically takes 3–6 months to show early results and 6–12 months for steady traffic growth. Location pages and GBP optimization can produce results in 30–60 days. Blog content and backlinks typically take 3–6 months to rank. The compounding effect means month 12 results are often 3–5x better than month 3. SEO is a long-term investment with the lowest cost per lead once established.</p>
<h3>What is the close rate for roofing leads?</h3>
<p>Close rates vary by source: referrals (40–60%), storm damage leads (30–50%), exclusive website leads (25–35%), Google Ads leads (25–35%), shared leads (5–15%). Response speed dramatically impacts close rates. The first contractor to respond wins 35–50% of competitive leads. Responding within 5 minutes makes you 21x more likely to qualify the lead than responding after 30 minutes.</p>
<h3>Should roofing companies use Facebook ads?</h3>
<p>Yes, but with the right strategy. Facebook ads work best for awareness, retargeting, and referral program promotion. They are less effective for immediate emergency leads than Google Ads. Target homeowners aged 35–65 in your service area. Use video creative showing before-and-after projects. Retarget website visitors with storm-specific offers. Expect $20–$80 cost per lead and 10–20% close rates.</p>
<h3>How do I market my roofing company in the winter?</h3>
<p>Focus on activities that build future pipeline: content creation for spring SEO, referral incentive campaigns, commercial bid preparation, interior restoration partnerships, and customer nurture sequences. Reduce ad spend on immediate-response campaigns and invest in systems that generate leads in March and April. Offer early-bird discounts for spring work booked in winter.</p>
<h3>What is the average roofing job value?</h3>
<p>The average residential roof replacement in the U.S. costs $9,200–$16,000, according to industry data. Asphalt shingle replacements typically range $8,000–$14,000. Metal roofs range $15,000–$30,000. Tile and slate can exceed $40,000. Commercial projects vary widely: $50,000–$500,000+ depending on square footage and system. Storm damage insurance jobs average $8,500–$25,000.</p>
<h3>Do door-knocking and canvassing still work for roofing?</h3>
<p>Yes, especially after storms. Door-knocking in neighborhoods with confirmed hail or wind damage generates leads at 25–35% contact-to-inspection rates. The key is speed — canvassers should hit affected areas within 24–48 hours of a storm. Combine door-knocking with digital follow-up (text, email) for maximum impact. Train canvassers thoroughly on compliance — some states regulate door-to-door solicitation after storms.</p>
<h3>How important are online reviews for roofing companies?</h3>
<p>Critical. 83% of homeowners trust online reviews as much as personal recommendations when choosing a roofer. Contractors with 50+ reviews and 4.5+ stars have a 57% higher chance of ranking in the top three map positions. A single one-star review drop can decrease conversion by 14%. Automated review generation systems typically produce 3–5x more reviews than manual requests.</p>
